<HOME> a reaction at temperature $T$ and pressure $P$, the equilibrium assemblage minimizes the total Gibbs energy, $G=\sum_i n_i\mu_i(T,P,\mathbf{x})$. This criterion does not specify how fast a phase nucleates. A glass, an interfacial melt, or a low-temperature intermediate can lower the kinetic barrier for a metastable product even when that product is not the minimum of $G$ \cite{schmelzer2020effects,mckenzie2020nucleation,bai2020kinetic}. Consequently, a phase-diagram-guided experiment must record both the nominal composition and the time--temperature path.
